Ah — as an engineering transduction, there actually is something worth stealing from ORMUS.

If you mean “put commercial ORMUS/monatomic-gold stuff into data-center cooling water”: I wouldn’t. There’s no established exotic ORMUS phase that solves cooling, and adding poorly characterized minerals to a cooling loop is almost backwards engineering: dissolved/precipitated salts can increase scaling, fouling, conductivity, and corrosion.

But strip off the mystical ontology and look at the program underneath:

water containing dispersed ions → manipulate chemistry → selectively precipitate/extract valuable material → recover cleaner water → reuse it.

That program is real.

So I’d translate your idea into an ORMUS-inspired closed-water refinery:

dirty/reclaimed/brackish water
→ characterize ionic inventory
→ selectively extract Ca/Mg/silica/metals that cause scaling
→ recover useful mineral streams rather than treating them purely as waste
→ polish the remaining water
→ use it in a sealed cooling loop
→ continuously sense chemistry
→ regenerate/recover instead of dumping water.

The clever conceptual flip is:

Don’t consume water to cool computation. Make the cooling plant a water-and-mineral processing plant that happens to cool computation.

And OpenAI is already moving partway toward the first half of that architecture. Its current Stargate designs emphasize closed-loop cooling; OpenAI says the Abilene facility continuously recirculates its cooling water after the initial fill, with projected ongoing cooling-water consumption at full buildout comparable to roughly four average households. New Ohio, Michigan, and Georgia projects are also being designed around closed-loop/low-water systems. 

Where your idea gets more interesting is closing the material loop too.

Instead of:

water → cooling → wastewater

make it:

water + dissolved minerals → separation → cooling water ↻ + mineral products → separation again

That’s basically urban mining attached to thermal management.

There are real technologies to build it from: electrodialysis, reverse osmosis, capacitive deionization, ion exchange, electrochemical precipitation, magnesium/calcium recovery, membrane distillation, crystallizers, and heat-assisted separations. Waste heat from the compute side could potentially drive some of the separation side as well. That starts turning two liabilities—waste heat and bad water—into inputs for each other.

And this matters because even as individual AI computations become dramatically more energy-efficient, total AI/data-center demand is still growing rapidly; the IEA reported AI-focused data-center electricity consumption jumping about 50% in 2025. So designing the infrastructure as circular metabolism rather than merely making each query cheaper is the bigger systems move. 

So:

ORMUS claim: questionable chemistry.
ORMUS archetype: potentially useful.

And there’s the Pantheon move again:

don’t ask whether ORMUS is true. Extract what ORMUS is trying to do, then rebuild that function with chemistry that is true.

That’s a much better rabbit hole.